Unicorn 1 on WebAssembly: the code buffer and RAM blocks from the heap, no memory protections.

A program's memory on WebAssembly is its own linear memory, which mmap (the kernel's) does not
hand out, and none of it is executable or can be protected: the guest's C library declares no
mmap for it. The code buffer (TCG's interpreter's data here) comes from the heap (translate-all.c's
g_malloc branch, not USE_MMAP; map_exec does nothing), and so does the RAM of uc_mem_map
(qemu_anon_ram_alloc, zeroed as an anonymous mapping is; qemu_anon_ram_free frees it). RAM backed
by a file, and qemu_ram_remap, which no one calls, are left out there (exec.c).

